content.text:
Your arrays page is up: https://claude.ai/artifact/7TVTSHjAtY6AvZjDztnq8q

It has ten sections, each with a Select button:
- the short answers;
- an array written and typed;
- syntax against library;
- how the sizes ride in the type;
- where the elements live today;
- boxing and its cost;
- what the rehearsal ran, and why its loop was slow;
- from here to an array program that runs;
- your design questions;
- the older reports, each marked current, partly out of date or out of date.

Every program on it ran today under the interpreter, and the page shows its real output. One of them shows the `double[]` store working when the program names it by hand, while the library's factory still picks the general store.

The writer found one new defect. Under the interpreter, `v: RR64^3 = [1.0 2.0 3.0]` binds, then `println(v)` stops with an internal error. I'll log it in the defect list once the batch has landed. I'll also commit the page's text to the repository then.

The review of ways to cut the transaction checks is still running.

Yes, the interpreter ran everything because no array has ever compiled on `main`. The compiled path reads only the prelude today, and the prelude has no array type: a program that names `Array1` stops with "Array1 is undefined". Library arrays have run compiled only in the rehearsal's throwaway tree. The only arrays the prelude ever had are its two hand-written vectors.

**What the brief asked for.** It quoted your questions and asked for teaching from small programs up:
- how an array is written and typed;
- syntax against library;
- how the sizes ride in the type, and how each path carries them;
- where the elements live on each path;
- what boxing costs;
- what the rehearsal ran and why it was slow;
- what this batch and the next bring;
- which questions are yours.

It told the writer to run every example under the interpreter, and for the compiled path to cite the record and claim no compiled run the record doesn't hold. The brief is in this conversation, not in the repository.

**What the page covers of the compiled path**, all by citation:
- Section 4: the class loader stamps sizes into each instance's class name, and writes a size in as a constant where a body reads it.
- Section 5: the compiled store is an `Object[]` of boxes, and the `double[]` class exists unused.
- Section 6: boxing, and why unboxed storage and unboxed reads are two separate decisions.
- Section 7: the four layers a compiled `a[i]` goes through.
- Section 8: the walls between here and a compiled array program that runs.
- Section 9: the five ways on file for giving a number array an unboxed store, and the ways for where unboxed arithmetic happens.

**What it doesn't do** is what you mainly asked: show how arrays carry over to the compiled path. It doesn't show which classes the compiler makes for an `Array1[\RR64, 0, 1000\]`, what bytecode a read and a write become, or what each unboxing way would change in that code. That's my brief's fault. I framed "how it works" around the interpreter, when your questions about the work and about boxing were about the compiled path.

**What I'd do:** a companion page on the compiled path alone, written by reading the code generator, the class loader and the run time. It would also rebuild the throwaway tree from the second probe's saved patch. That shows the real classes and bytecode of `a[i]` and `a[i] := v` with real output, and then what each unboxing way would change in them. It would start after run 1 lands, so its build doesn't slow the batch's full check. I'd guess about 1M to 1.5M tokens written.

Shall I start that page when run 1 lands?

