Yes to both. I've started it now, and the compiled path goes into the same page as its second half.

Your point about directories is right. The writer works in its own throwaway worktree, so it touches nothing the landing uses. The shared part is the machine: 4 CPUs and about 15 GB of memory, with no swap. The batch's full check is running on it right now. A full build beside it could push the full check's checker stage past its time limit, or run the machine out of memory. Either one would fail the full check.

So the writer does its reading and writing now, and builds its throwaway tree only when I tell it the full check has ended. Then it:
- compiles the one library and the array and dot programs against it;
- shows the real bytecode of `a[i]` and `a[i] := v`, layer by layer, down to the store;
- shows where the boxes are made and where the JIT can remove them;
- says what each unboxing way would change in those classes and that code.

It will also rewrite the page's short answers to cover both paths. I'll republish at the same link when it's done.

